{{also|Whistleblower|whistleblower}}

==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|whistleblower|whistle blower}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|whistle|blower}}, referring to a [[police officer]] blowing their whistle on observing a violation of the law.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-whistle-blower.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang|chiefly|US}} One who [[report]]s a [[problem]] or [[violation]] to the [[authorities]]; especially, an [[employee]] or former employee who reports a violation by an [[employer]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:informant}}
#: {{ux|en|We owe it to a small group of brave '''whistle-blowers''' that we know about the infractions at all.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Frederick D. Lipman|title=Whistleblowers|year=2012|publisher=Wiley|location=Hoboken, N.J.|page=60|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/whistleblowersin0000lipm/page/60/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-118-09403-7|passage=Usually the '''whistleblower''' is not fired outright. The organization's goal is to disconnect the act of whistleblowing from the act of retaliation, which is why so much legislation to protect '''whistleblowers''' is practically irrelevant. The usual practice is to demoralize and humiliate the '''whistleblower''', putting him or her under so much psychological stress that it becomes difficult to do a good job.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ben Smith|title=The Intercept Promised to Reveal Everything|work=The New York Times|date=2020-09-14|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/09/13/business/media/the-intercept-source-reality-winner.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=The huge breach of the National Security Agency’s domestic surveillance program in June 2013 was one of the proudest moments in modern journalism, and one of the purest: A brave and disgusted '''whistle-blower''', Edward Snowden, revealed the government’s extensive surveillance of American and foreign citizens.}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|whistleblowing}}

